//! Event queues (Phase 17, ADR 0353).
//!
//! An event queue watches many descriptors at once and hands out what
//! became of them, as BSD's kqueue does: a program adds a watch for each
//! descriptor and way it cares about, and `wait` returns the watches that
//! are ready, however many others wait idle. radix-process(2)'s `create`
//! makes one (ObjectKind event_queue), and the C library's kqueue(2) and
//! kevent(2) are its interface (radix-posix(7)).
//!
//! A watch is named by its `ident` and `filter`, and a queue holds at most
//! one of each pair. The `read` and `write` filters watch the descriptor
//! `ident` (the slot less `wait`'s `base`): `read` is ready while it can be
//! read without waiting or has reached its end, and `write` while it can
//! be written without waiting or no longer reads what is written. Pipes,
//! sockets, terminals and the devices that `poll` waits for can be
//! watched, as can notifications; files and directories, which are always
//! ready, cannot, as with Linux's epoll. The `user` filter watches an
//! event of the program's own, numbered `ident`, which a change with
//! USER_TRIGGER makes ready.
//!
//! A watch holds the description its descriptor named when it was added,
//! not the slot: it lasts until it is deleted, until a oneshot watch is
//! reported, until another description is added with its `ident` and
//! `filter`, until the queue goes, or until the description itself goes,
//! when the last of its descriptors in every process closes. A description
//! closed in this process but open in another, after `fork`, is still
//! watched, as on Linux and unlike BSD, until the watch is deleted or its
//! descriptor number added again.
//!
//! A watch is level-triggered: each `wait` reports it again while it is
//! ready. With `clear` it is edge-triggered instead: reported once, then
//! again only after its descriptor changes. Events of a ready descriptor
//! are reported once a call, in the order the watches became ready.
//! Several threads may wait on one queue: each event goes to one of them.
//! A copy of the capability, as `fork` makes, is the same queue.

module event_queue;

/// What a watch watches: kqueue's filters, whose numbers are negative in
/// the C library's 16-bit `filter`.
enum QueueFilter: u16 {
    /// A descriptor that can be read, or has reached its end
    /// (EVFILT_READ, -1).
    read = 0xffff;
    /// A descriptor that can be written, or reads no more
    /// (EVFILT_WRITE, -2).
    write = 0xfffe;
    /// An event of the program's own (EVFILT_USER, -11).
    user = 0xfff5;
    /// A descriptor's PollEvents, as Linux's epoll watches them (-32,
    /// which kqueue does not use). A change's `fflags` holds the events
    /// asked for; an event's, those found of them, with error and
    /// hang_up, which are always reported. (Since version 2, ADR 0612.)
    poll = 0xffe0;
}

/// What a change does, and what an event says, as kqueue's EV_ flags: a
/// QueueEvent's `flags`, in 16 bits.
flags QueueFlags: u32 {
    /// Adds the watch, or changes the value and user flags of the one
    /// with its `ident` and `filter`, whose `oneshot`, `clear` and
    /// `dispatch` stay as they were added (EV_ADD), but for a `poll`
    /// watch, which every change that names it gives its events,
    /// `oneshot`, `clear` and `dispatch` anew, as Linux's EPOLL_CTL_MOD. Either way the watch
    /// is enabled, unless the change also has `disable` and not `enable`.
    add = 0;
    /// Deletes the watch (EV_DELETE).
    delete = 1;
    /// Lets the watch be reported again (EV_ENABLE). It wins over
    /// `disable` in the same change.
    enable = 2;
    /// Keeps the watch but reports it no more until it is enabled
    /// (EV_DISABLE).
    disable = 3;
    /// Deletes the watch once it is reported (EV_ONESHOT).
    oneshot = 4;
    /// Reports the watch only when what it watches changes, not for as
    /// long as it is ready (EV_CLEAR); a user event is reset once
    /// reported.
    clear = 5;
    /// Reports the change itself as an event, with `error` and the status
    /// in `data`, 0 when it worked (EV_RECEIPT).
    receipt = 6;
    /// Disables the watch once it is reported (EV_DISPATCH).
    dispatch = 7;
    /// With `add`, of a `poll` watch: when its descriptor changes, the
    /// change readies only one of the exclusive watches of that
    /// description, in this queue or another, with the non-exclusive ones,
    /// as Linux's EPOLLEXCLUSIVE, so that one of many waiting threads is
    /// woken. Valid only with `add`, and not for a watch the queue holds.
    /// (Since version 2, ADR 0612.)
    exclusive = 10;
    /// With `add`: fails with exists if the queue holds a watch of the
    /// `ident` and `filter` for the description the descriptor names, as
    /// Linux's EPOLL_CTL_ADD; one for another description is replaced, as
    /// without it. (Since version 2, ADR 0612.)
    create = 11;
    /// In an event: the change failed, with the kernel's status in `data`
    /// (EV_ERROR).
    error = 14;
    /// In an event: the descriptor has reached its end, or has failed, or
    /// its other end has gone (EV_EOF).
    eof = 15;
}

/// In a `user` change's `fflags`: makes the event ready (NOTE_TRIGGER).
const USER_TRIGGER: u32 = 0x01000000;
/// In a `user` change's `fflags`: the low 24 bits are the event's own
/// flags, which `USER_CONTROL` says what to do with (NOTE_FFLAGSMASK).
const USER_FLAGS: u32 = 0x00ffffff;
/// In a `user` change's `fflags`: the two bits that say how the change's
/// own flags apply to the event's (NOTE_FFCTRLMASK): neither leaves them
/// as they are, USER_AND keeps those in both, USER_OR adds them, and both
/// together (USER_COPY) replace them.
const USER_CONTROL: u32 = 0xc0000000;
/// Keeps the event's flags that are also the change's (NOTE_FFAND).
const USER_AND: u32 = 0x40000000;
/// Adds the change's flags to the event's (NOTE_FFOR).
const USER_OR: u32 = 0x80000000;
/// Replaces the event's flags with the change's (NOTE_FFCOPY).
const USER_COPY: u32 = 0xc0000000;

/// One change `wait` makes, or one event it reports: the C library's
/// `struct kevent`.
struct QueueEvent {
    /// A descriptor for `read` and `write`; any number for `user`.
    ident: u64;
    /// The QueueFilter, as 16 bits.
    filter: u16;
    /// QueueFlags.
    flags: u16;
    /// A user event's flags (see USER_TRIGGER); a `poll` watch's
    /// PollEvents; 0 for the other filters.
    fflags: u32;
    /// In a change of a user event: the value its events report. In an
    /// event: that value for a user event, the status of a change that
    /// failed (`error`), and 0 otherwise.
    data: i64;
    /// The program's own value: each event carries the one its watch was
    /// last added with.
    udata: u64;
}

/// How `wait` waits.
flags QueueWaitFlags: u32 {
    /// Wait with `mask` as the calling thread's signal mask, and put the
    /// old one back after, as `ppoll` does.
    mask = 0;
    /// Wait as long as it takes: `timeout_ns` is ignored.
    forever = 1;
}

/// The most changes one `wait` takes: as many as the most descriptors a
/// process may have, twice over.
const CHANGES_MAX: u32 = 8192;

/// An event queue.
interface event_queue = 0x0034 version 2 {
    kind = event_queue;
    /// Makes the `count` changes at `changes`, in order, then, if
    /// `capacity` is not 0, waits until a watch is ready, at most
    /// `timeout_ns` nanoseconds (unless forever; 0 does not wait), and
    /// writes up to `capacity` events at `events`. Returns how many
    /// events it wrote. A descriptor's `ident` is its slot less `base`.
    ///
    /// A change of filter 0 is passed over. A change with neither add
    /// nor delete changes the value and enabling of a watch the queue
    /// holds, and makes a user event ready with USER_TRIGGER; `add`
    /// together with `delete` is invalid. A change that fails, and one
    /// that asks for a receipt, is reported as an event with the error
    /// flag and its status (or 0) in `data`, while `capacity` has room;
    /// once it has none, `wait` stops there, and fails with the status of
    /// a change that failed, or returns the events written before a
    /// receipt. When any such event was written, `wait` returns them and
    /// reports no watch. A change can fail with
    /// invalid_argument (an unknown filter, add with delete, or a watch
    /// of an event queue), empty_slot or no_such_slot (an add for a
    /// descriptor that is not open), not_permitted (an add for a file or
    /// directory, which is always ready), not_found (a watch the queue
    /// does not hold, other than by add, or a `poll` watch whose
    /// descriptor names another description now), exists (an add with
    /// `create` of a watch the queue holds) and no_memory.
    ///
    /// Fails with interrupted if a signal comes while it waits, with
    /// bad_address if `changes` or `events` cannot be read or written,
    /// and with invalid_argument if `count` is more than CHANGES_MAX.
    /// Making changes needs the write right, and waiting for events the
    /// read right. (Since Phase 17, ADR 0353.)
    method wait = 1 (changes: addr, count: u32, events: addr, capacity: u32, base: u32,
                     timeout_ns: u64, mask: u64, flags: QueueWaitFlags) -> (found: u32)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                not_permitted, not_found, exists, no_memory, bad_address, interrupted];
}
